---
title: 'ModMox: Encounter-Enabled'
blurb: The debug panel is gone, cards fly between zones, the game taps your lands for you, and Emblems and Potions arrive. A full encounter is now playable from opening hand to last point of damage.
date: 2026-11-13
tags: [game]
---

At the end of the [last post](/blog/modmox-gets-an-opponent), ModMox was a game you
could play a round of if you didn't mind answering half the questions through a debug
panel full of lists. Mulligans went through the panel, and so did casting a spell.
Nothing moved, either. The board just blinked from one state to the next, and the
Villain took its whole turn faster than you could read it.

Only two days later, the GUI milestone is closed, the debug panel has been deleted, and the
Magic part of the game is a joy! A whole encounter now plays out the way you'd expect it to.

The work fell into five chunks:

## Every decision gets a real input

The rule from last time was that a kind of decision isn't finished until its debug
form is deleted. Now, that rule has been rendered fully moot. Cards are cast, or played as lands, by
dragging them out of your hand or double-clicking them, and a single click does
nothing. Your hand stays in whatever order you drag it into, and a canceled cast puts
the card back exactly where it was.

The stack got its own window. Each spell or ability on it gets a color for as long as
it's there, and hovering an entry marks its source and its targets on the board in
that color. That needed something to target on the stack, so Counterspell joined the
card pool, followed by Raise Dead, the first card that targets a graveyard, and
Stifle, the first card from outside Alpha and the first that counters an ability.
Clicking any pile now opens a window onto it, libraries included, with a card back for
every card you don't know. The engine keeps track of which cards you do know, so the
one you put on the bottom during a mulligan is sitting there face up.

The pregame got an overlay of its own, with your opening hand drawn large and buttons
that read things like "Keep 6" and "Free Mulligan." Once that landed, the debug panel
had nothing left to answer and was removed. There's now a test that fails if the
engine ever gains a kind of decision with no input, so it can't quietly come back.

## A game you can follow

The most useful change for actually reading the board is that rules text is no longer
written by me. It's generated from each card's abilities, in current Oracle wording,
every time the card is drawn. That was always going to be necessary, because a card a
player has modified needs text nobody typed. The cost is that some real wordings are
out of reach. Howling Mine says "that player draws a card" instead of "an additional
card," because nothing in its abilities says "additional." I'll take plainly correct
over word for word. Anything the generator has no words for prints as a class name in
square brackets, and a test walks every card and fails on a bracket.

Then things started moving. The Encounter Screen still throws its board away and
rebuilds it from every snapshot, and I didn't want to give that up, because it's why
the screen can't get out of sync with the game. So animation sits on top. A small
object outside the redrawn board remembers where each permanent's face was last
drawn, and after a rebuild each face eases from there to where it belongs. A redraw in
the middle of a move is harmless, since the new face just picks up where the old one
had gotten to. Undo doesn't animate at all, because nothing happened in the game. Cards
that change zones fly there, turning from one kind of face into the other on the way.

My first try timed the moves off the wall clock, and every one of them started with a
jump. The frame that rebuilds the board is a slow one, and it was eating the first
chunk of every animation. Now time is counted a frame at a time, and no frame is
allowed to count for more than a thirtieth of a second.

The Villain also got slowed down. Its next answer is held back until the last one has
been on screen long enough to read, about half a second after an action and less after
tapping a land, and the time it spent thinking counts toward the wait. One Game Speed
setting scales every wait, move and fade together, from Slow up to Blazing.

For reading a card properly there's the Inspector. Right-click a card and it takes
over the sidebar with the full card, a glow in the color of its rarity, everything
currently affecting it, and a reminder for each keyword. I built a version that
followed the cursor first, and it was too busy over the hand, so now it only shows
what you pin. The board also stopped being gray. There are pixel art backdrops behind
it and portraits on the player panels, a white wizard for you and a purple demon for
the Villain, all placeholders until the real art exists.

## Less clicking

Paying for every spell by clicking lands one at a time was only ever a first step, so
the game does it now. Auto-tap picks your lands using the same payment planner the AI uses for its
own. I considered a Pay button to confirm the plan, and rejected it for adding a click
to every spell to guard against a rare mistake. If you care which lands get tapped,
tap them first, because mana already in your pool is spent before anything else. And
before you commit, hovering a card shows a Tap Preview where only the sources that
would be used keep their glow.

Sharing the planner paid off right away. It turned out the AI would happily tap
Llanowar Elves for green while an untapped Forest sat next to it. It reaches for a
land first now, and fixing that for me fixed it for the Villain too.

A few smaller things came with it. Yield Turn passes every priority until the next
turn begins, holding Ctrl stops the game from passing anything for you, and pass,
undo, cancel and hold can each be rebound to any key.

## An AI that stops wasting its mana

The Villain had a bad habit that got fixed along the way. It would activate an ability
every time it had spare mana, stacked on top of itself, even when that accomplished
nothing. It would give one creature flying four times in a row.

Three things were conspiring. The board evaluator counted a keyword once for every
time it had been granted, so four helpings of flying looked four times as good. The
one-step lookahead scored the board while an "until end of turn" effect was still
there, so any pump beat passing. And by the end of a longer rollout the effect had
worn off, everything tied, and the tie went to the first instinct.

Now a keyword counts once, an ability is never activated on top of itself, and when a
combat trick is on the table every option gets played out to the end of the turn
before it's scored. A trick only wins by leaving something that lasts, like damage
dealt or a creature saved. The AI also weighs activating an ability two, four or eight
times as a single plan, so a pump that takes two activations to win a fight isn't
dismissed for looking useless after one. Last time the search changed the AI's mind in
about 5% of decisions. In the new benchmark Normal answered differently from Easy in
30 of 190, and in nearly all of those Easy activated something and Normal passed.

## Emblems and Potions

Milestone 14 is the first one that points at the roguelike. Emblems already existed as
the way a character's lasting effects reach the engine, and now they can have
abilities you activate. The two test Boons are Diviner's Boon, which draws two cards
at sorcery speed, and Electromancer's Boon, which deals 3 damage to any target. Each
works once per game.

"Once per game" is a restriction on the ability, the way the rules treat it, and not a
cost. The difference shows up when someone responds. An Emblem's ability goes on the
stack like any other, so Stifle can counter it, and the ability is spent either way.

An Emblem is never used up, though. What gets used up is a Potion, a new kind of
object whose single ability has "use up this Potion" as part of its cost. The mock one
is called Big Gulp and gives a creature +2/+2 until end of turn. Making it an ability
instead of a spell meant it needed no card to be, no zone to be cast from, and nowhere
to go afterward. It also gives a nice rules answer for free: Stifle counters a Potion,
Counterspell doesn't, and the Potion is gone regardless, because costs are paid first.

## Where it stands

The card pool is at 44 cards, there are 36 decision records, and the count of test
functions has passed 2,000, most of them game-side. Every decision in an encounter
has a real input, and nothing in the encounter is waiting on a debug tool.

So the next milestone finally leaves the battlefield. It's the Deckbuilder, where
you'll build decks from your collection and apply Mods to cards, three slots to a
card. A prototype of it is built and waiting on me to sign off. The run around it is
designed on paper too, with a map for each stage, stores, workshops, and Hearts to
lose, all derived from a single seed. None of that is code yet. But the thing a run is
made of, one fight against one opponent, finally plays like a game.
